// Lore for the Aqshy map. See src/data/lore/index.js.
//
// Aqshy is the Realm of Fire, and the Great Parch is the part of it anyone has
// bothered to chart: a continent of ash plains, volcanic chains and warm seas
// where the Free Cities of Order, the Goretide, the Fyreslayer lodges and the
// Skaven of the Gnaw have all been fighting over the same handful of roads,
// harbours and realmgates since the Age of Chaos ended. Entries say what a
// place is, what the plate actually shows of it, and why an army would march
// there. Where the record is thin, so is the entry.
export const AQSHY_LORE = {
  // --- The realm ----------------------------------------------------------
  aqshy: `Aqshy is the Realm of Fire, and the Great Parch is its charted heart: a continent where the rock still remembers being molten, the rivers run red with suspended ash, and the seas are warm enough to sail in winter and warm enough to kill in summer. Everything on this map is either built on old lava or built to survive it.

Fire makes Aqshy valuable rather than merely dangerous. Its forges never want for heat, its realmstone burns in the hand, and the passions of its people are proverbial across the eight realms — which is why Sigmar planted his first and grandest Free Cities here, and why Khorne’s warhosts have never stopped coming to take them.

No power holds the whole Parch. Order holds the cities and the roads between them, the Goretide holds the ash in the south, the Skaven hold what is underneath, and the wild interior holds itself. The map is a record of where those four have most recently stopped pushing.`,

  // --- Cotha and the north-western shore ----------------------------------
  'vinewrought-gate': `A realmgate on Cotha’s northern coast, framed in fused black creeper that no fire has managed to burn off, opening onto somewhere green. Cotha’s traders will tell you nothing about what comes through it, which is itself informative.

The gate is the reason Cotha’s neutrality has a price. A landmass that owns a working passage to another realm does not get to stay uninteresting, however hard it tries.`,

  cotha: 'A hard-bitten, snow-rimed landmass on the Great Parch’s north-western edge, between the Ocean of Swords and the Searing Sea, its walled market grown rich on caravan trade between the coastal Free Cities and the interior tribes. Its neutrality is a business model rather than a principle, and Cotha will sell to whichever side is currently winning.\n\nThat neutrality nearly cost it everything once: a frozen realmgate to Ghyran hidden at Cotha’s heart let Nurgle pour daemon legions through it, and the Plague Fleet that sailed from its shores went on to shatter Bataar’s navy in a week of fighting. Cotha has never fully explained how it survived hosting an invasion it did not start, and its trading partners have learned not to ask too closely.',

  'armadas-bane': `A scatter of black rocks barely breaking the swell off Cotha’s western approaches, unmarked by any light and charted by every navigator who survived meeting them. The name is not a boast; it is a count.

Ships still take the inside passage to save three days, and the wrecks on the rocks are what the salvage crews of the Ocean of Swords live on.`,

  'betrayer-fjords': `Deep, narrow inlets biting into Cotha’s southern flank, sheltered enough to hide a fleet and awkward enough that a fleet hidden there cannot leave quickly. Corsairs out of the Ocean of Swords winter here when the Searing Sea gets too hot to work.

The name predates anyone willing to explain it, which on this coast usually means the story is still politically awkward.`,

  'cape-knife': `A thin blade of rock reaching out from Cotha into the strait, its seaward edge sheared off clean as if cut. Every vessel bound between the Ocean of Swords and the Searing Sea rounds it, and everyone who wants to tax that traffic has thought about fortifying it.

Nobody has managed to keep a garrison on the Knife for long. There is no fresh water on it and no way to land supplies in a blow.`,

  'crackling-coast': `A shoreline of fractured volcanic glass that shatters and re-forms with the tide, named for the noise it makes at low water. It is impossible to walk on in soft boots and worse to beach a boat on.

That makes it the one stretch of Cotha’s southern shore nobody bothers to watch, which is exactly why smugglers land here.`,

  'isle-of-trysts': `A low green island in the strait, neutral ground by long custom, where captains and chieftains who cannot safely meet on anyone’s territory meet instead. No weapons are carried ashore and no quarrel begun on the isle is ever finished there.

The custom holds because everyone finds it useful. It has held through three Chaos invasions, which says more about the value of somewhere to talk than about anyone’s honour.`,

  // --- Bataar and the Ocean of Swords -------------------------------------
  'flamespider-woad': `A stretch of scrub on Bataar’s northern uplands where the firesilk spiders nest, spinning thread that will not burn. Harvesting it is dangerous, skilled and extremely profitable, and the clans who do it guard their grounds like mines.

Bataar’s wealth begins here. Half the fireproof cloth in the Great Parch comes off these slopes, and the cities that buy it have all at some point considered taking the woad instead.`,

  'venal-peaks': `A ridge of mineral-rich mountains along Bataar’s spine, worked by clan forges that sell to anyone and swear to no one. The ore is good, the smelters are old, and the prices move with the war news.

Nothing about the Venal Peaks is loyal. That is understood by everyone who buys from them, and factored in.`,

  'lake-of-dark-pacts': `A still, black tarn on the Bataari plain where the water reflects nothing, and where the clans have traditionally gone to swear the oaths they would rather no god overheard. The water does not ripple and does not freeze.

Whatever listens at the lake has never been named on any map. Bataar’s chieftains keep going anyway, because an oath sworn there is one nobody in the confederation will break.`,

  bataar: 'A confederation of horse-warrior clans and merchant-kings ranging the open plains and coasts west of Vitrolia and Golvaria, loyal to no Free City and answerable only to its own chieftains. Its western cities grew rich enough to rival Aspiria’s Wizard Lords in all but war, the two great powers settling their rivalry through trade, negotiation and athletic competition rather than the sword.\n\nBataar’s wealth was built on firesilk and the mineral forges of the Venal Peaks, and carried further still by the Floating City, a seaborne trading market the wizard lords of Aspiria once enchanted to fly. Bataar’s riders trade as readily as they raid, and which one a caravan gets usually depends on how well it bargains.',

  'forests-of-kindling': `A drowned forest off Bataar’s western shore, its petrified trunks still standing in the shallows where the coast dropped away. The wood burns hot enough to melt iron and is quarried rather than felled.

Bataar sells kindling from here to every forge on the Parch that can afford it, and the clans that hold the shallows have fought each other over them for longer than the Free Cities have existed.`,

  newvassal: `A Bataari market town, walled and prosperous until it was not, now a ring of foundations and a burned gate. Nobody on the plate admits to having done it, which narrows the list of candidates considerably.

The wells still work. Caravans water at Newvassal and move on the same day, because the clans have a superstition about sleeping inside its walls.`,

  'nexus-aqshia': `A junction of Bataari roads where four clan territories meet at a single waymarked crossing, kept open by a truce older than the confederation itself. Anyone may pass; nobody may camp; blood spilled at the Nexus is answered by every clan at once.

It is the closest thing Bataar has to a capital, and the fact that it is a crossroads rather than a city tells you everything about how Bataar governs itself.`,

  'floating-city': 'Bataar’s Floating City began as an ordinary seaborne trading market, built by the merchant lords to carry their wares across the Ocean of Swords, until a deal struck with the Wizard Lords of Aspiria saw it enchanted to fly outright.\n\nIt drifts on currents and old magic now rather than any engine, which makes the Floating City nearly impossible to besiege and only slightly easier to trade with, since it rarely lingers above the same stretch of coastline for long. Merchants who want its cargo have learned to watch the sky as closely as the horizon.',

  'brokers-bay': `A sheltered bight on Bataar’s southern coast where the clans meet the sea traders, with no town and no customs house, only a beach and a season. For two months a year it is the busiest market in the west; the rest of the time it is empty sand.

Everyone involved prefers it that way. A market with no walls is a market nobody can seize.`,

  'bright-mountains': `A range of pale, quartz-veined peaks south of Bataar that throw back the light hard enough to blind at noon. Brightspear took its name from them and its first sigmarite from their flanks.

The passes are the only way inland from Brightspear’s coast. Whoever holds them decides whether the city trades with the interior or only with the sea.`,

  'the-bloodied-track': `An old caravan road running east out of Bataar towards Golvaria, named for the colour the dust turns after a raid rather than for any single battle. It is the shortest route between the western clans and the Golvarian ports, and the most frequently ambushed.

Merchants still use it. The alternative adds a month, and a month is worth more than the risk to anyone carrying firesilk.`,

  // --- Aspiria and the southern Ocean of Swords ---------------------------
  'disintegrating-shores': `A coastline that is actively falling apart, the cliffs shedding into the Ocean of Swords fast enough that charts go out of date within a decade. Aspiria’s wizards study the collapse; nobody has stopped it.

It makes the western approach to Aspiria effectively unassailable. No fleet is going to land on a beach that may not be there tomorrow.`,

  brightspear: 'Brightspear is one of the Free Cities of the Great Parch, its skyline dominated by a single vast spire of forged sigmarite raised as beacon and boast in equal measure, said to be visible from the Scablands on a clear night.\n\nRaised only decades after Hammerhal itself, it grew to match the older Free Cities in grandeur far faster than its youth should have allowed, thanks to trade rather than conquest: the Floating City of Bataar sails across the Great Parch to sell to it, tribes from the wild interior come in to bargain, and corsairs out of Defiance Cove find willing buyers for their grislier catches among Brightspear’s wizards and alchemists.\n\nThat commerce is Brightspear’s real fortification as much as its walls are, binding half the Great Parch’s disparate powers to a city none of them can afford to see fall. Brightspear’s light is a landmark for the lost and a target for anyone who resents the reminder, but so long as the caravans keep arriving, the reminder keeps burning.',

  'agloraxian-citadels': `The ground-bound remnants of the Agloraxi mage-empire, a chain of towers and vaults the Arch-Domini left behind when they took to the sky. Most are sealed, some are collapsed, and a handful still answer to the right word spoken at the right door.

Aspiria’s Wizard Lords claim the Citadels by right of proximity and have never managed to open more than a fraction of them. Every generation a new expedition goes in, and every generation the survivors come out with something the last lot missed.`,

  'isle-of-exiles': `A bare island off Aspiria’s coast where the Wizard Lords put those they will neither pardon nor execute. There is no garrison and no wall; the current does the work.

Exiles are landed with tools and left. Some of what has grown up there over the centuries is no longer entirely willing to be collected.`,

  aspiria: 'A cluster of terraced settlements climbing a long volcanic ridge, its farmers coaxing crops from ash-rich soil that would kill lesser plantings. What Aspiria lacks in walls it makes up for in stubbornness, having outlasted three raids by simply refusing to burn as expected.',

  lumnos: 'A modest trading town on the Great Parch’s inland roads, notable mainly for the shrine its founders raised on the one hill for miles that fire never quite reaches. Caravans stop here for the same reason armies do: it is reliably safe ground between two worse places.',

  'passions-gate': `A pass through Aspiria’s southern ridge, narrow enough for two carts abreast, where the realm’s heat runs strongest and tempers with it. Travellers are advised to cross it in company and not to argue on the way through.

Aspiria keeps a toll post at either end. The tolls are modest; the real business is counting who goes in and who comes out.`,

  'ocean-of-swords': `The western sea, named for the blade-thin basalt stacks that break its surface for leagues off every shore. It is navigable, barely, by anyone with a chart and a shallow draught, which is why Bataar and Aspiria both maintain fleets of small, fast, expendable hulls rather than anything grand.

The Ocean of Swords is Aqshy’s back door. Every invasion of the western Parch has come across it, and every one of them lost ships to the stacks before it lost any to the defenders.`,

  'the-aspirids': `A chain of low islands trailing south from Aspiria, settled by families who wanted the Wizard Lords’ protection without their attention. Each isle is effectively its own village, and they have been intermarrying and feuding for centuries.

Aspiria counts them as territory. The Aspirids count themselves as Aspirids, and pay their dues late.`,

  'lucid-coast': `A stretch of the southern shore where the water goes glass-clear over pale sand, so transparent that the wrecks on the bottom are visible from the deck. It is the one pleasant beach on this coast and the most thoroughly picked-over.

Divers work it all season. What they bring up is mostly ballast and occasionally something the Agloraxi dropped.`,

  'vanx-littoral': `The northern shore of Vanx, a low, damp margin of reed flats and mud where the Ocean of Swords loses its temper and simply seeps inland. There is no harbour and no need of one.

Armies avoid it. Anything heavier than a mule sinks, which is the Littoral’s entire strategic contribution and a sufficient one.`,

  'shattered-fortress-isles': `A cluster of islets in the southern ocean, each carrying the broken stump of a fortification nobody now claims to have built. The masonry is older than the Free Cities and better cut than anything raised since.

Aspiria’s scholars argue about who built them. The corsairs who shelter in the ruins have never cared, so long as the walls still break the wind.`,

  'wreckers-isle': `A single rock off the Vanx coast, occupied by families who have made a living for generations out of what the sea brings them and, when the sea is slow, out of encouraging it. The lights on the headland are not where a chart says lights should be.

Every power in the south has threatened to burn the isle out. None has, because the Wreckers know these waters and a pilot who knows these waters is worth more than a grievance.`,

  'bleached-forest': `A stand of pale, sun-killed timber inland of the Vanx coast, dead for so long that the wood has gone hard as stone and will no longer burn. Vanx’s people quarry it for beams and boat frames.

It is the only building material for a hundred leagues, which makes a forest of dead trees the most valuable ground in the region.`,

  'serried-peaks': `A wall of close-packed mountains on the far south-western edge of the plate, running off the chart’s margin into country nobody has surveyed. They mark where the Great Parch stops being mapped.

What is on the other side is a matter of rumour. Expeditions that go looking come back reduced or not at all, and Vanx treats the Peaks as a border rather than an obstacle.`,

  vanx: `The far southern province, a dry, thinly held country of scrub, dead forest and salt flats between the Serried Peaks and the sea. Its people farm what they can, salvage what they can, and are left alone by everyone because there is nothing here worth the march.

That isolation is Vanx’s single asset. When the Parch burns, the refugees go south, and Vanx has absorbed more of them than its soil can comfortably feed.`,

  // --- Golvaria and the Vitrolian coast -----------------------------------
  'resinwood-coast': `Golvaria’s northern shore, thick with a sap-heavy jungle that bleeds flammable resin from every cut trunk. The defoliant mists that keep Anvilgard’s streets clear were invented for this coast.

The resin is worth shipping and the forest resents being harvested. Logging camps here are fortified, and the ones that are not become brief.`,

  'the-cinderfloes': `Rafts of pumice and cooled slag drifting on the northern Searing Sea, some of them acres across and stable enough to walk on. They wander with the current and are never in the same place twice.

Navigators hate them and salvage crews love them, because anything that sinks in the northern sea eventually washes up on a floe.`,

  'vale-of-cursed-beasts': `A valley in the Golvarian highlands where the realm’s wildlife has gone badly wrong, the herds twisted into something that hunts in packs and does not flee fire. Golvaria’s settlements maintain a cordon rather than a campaign.

Nobody has established what curdled the Vale, though the Agloraxi ruins upslope are the usual suspect. The cordon holds, which is all the local lords have ever promised.`,

  'skyheim-peaks': `The mountain wall that divides Golvaria’s coast from its interior, high enough to catch snow in a realm that barely acknowledges the concept. Duardin airships out of Tempest’s Eye use the range as a navigation mark from half the Parch away.

Two passes cross it. Both are tolled, both are garrisoned, and both have changed hands often enough that the tollhouses are built like keeps.`,

  golvaria: 'The strait and shoreline that give the Golvarian Passage its name, the only safe sea lane between the Great Parch’s Free Cities and the wider realm. Anvilgard built its fortune watching this water, and anyone hoping to challenge that fortune has to take Golvaria first.',

  'castle-drakesbane': `A fortress on the Golvarian coast raised, according to its own garrison, on the spot where a drake was killed, and named to make sure nobody forgets it. The bones are gone; the claim is maintained.

It watches the coast road and the inland approach to Hallowheart both, which is reason enough to keep it manned whatever the truth of the story.`,

  'fury-strait': `The narrow water between Cotha and the Golvarian mainland, where two currents meet head-on and neither yields. Ships transit it at slack tide or not at all.

It is the shortest route between the northern and western seas, and the reason Cape Knife and Castle Drakesbane both exist. Close the Strait and the western Parch has to go the long way round.`,

  'vendetta-bridge': `A stone span across a Golvarian gorge, built by one family and burned by another so many times that the current bridge is the seventh of its name. The feud is documented on the parapet, which is longer than it sounds.

Both families still maintain it, because neither will let the other have the only crossing for forty leagues. The vendetta is, in effect, load-bearing.`,

  'hags-delta': `Where the Vitrolian rivers spill into the Wretch Sea through a fan of silt channels that shift every season. Charts of the Delta are sold with an expiry date.

The channels hide anything with a shallow draught, which suits the smugglers working between Hallowheart and the coast and inconveniences everyone trying to stop them.`,

  'wretch-sea': `A warm inland sea between Golvaria and Vitrolia, shallow, sulphurous and crowded with traffic because the overland alternative crosses Vitrolia. The water corrodes hulls and nobody has found a paint that stops it.

Every barge on the Wretch Sea is on its last season, and every barge master is calculating whether one more crossing pays for the next hull.`,

  hallowhart: 'Hallowheart is a fortified mining city built around the Shimmering Abyss, a colossal pit sunk into the rock of Golvaria in the Great Parch, its walls and caverns crusted with precious gems and the raw realmstone that wizards of every college come to study.\n\nThe Hallowed Knights founded it in the years after the Realmgate Wars, once they and Fyreslayers of the Vostarg lodge had slain the Wyrdflame Drake, a serpent of living flame that Tzeentch’s touch had corrupted and set to guard the pit. What they cleared, they kept: Hallowheart grew from the fortified camps that followed the drake’s death into one of the Great Parch’s own Free Cities, its wizards drawn from every arcane tradition rather than one.\n\nThe realmstone hauled up from the dark is worth more than the city raised to guard it, and every college of magic with an interest in raw power still sends agents to bid for a claim on the Abyss’s veins. That rivalry keeps Hallowheart wealthy and watchful in equal measure, since a pit this valuable never quite runs out of people willing to dig it.',

  'the-gatelands': `The country west of Vitrolia where a dozen minor realmgates open within a few days’ ride of one another, most of them unstable and some of them one-way. Wizards survey them; nobody settles among them.

A concentration of gates like this ought to be a strategic prize. In practice it is a hazard, because an army that camps in the Gatelands can wake up somewhere else.`,

  vitrolia: 'A stretch of desert fused into black glass by some long-ago firestorm, sharp enough underfoot to cut through cheap boots. It is worthless as land, which is exactly why armies march around it rather than fight over better-defended ground nearby.',

  'kindling-forests': `Dry woodland on Vitrolia’s southern flank that has burned to the root and grown back so many times that the cycle is now measured and expected. The local settlements plant to the burn schedule.

An enemy who fires the Kindling Forests out of season destroys a harvest. One who fires them in season achieves nothing at all, which the Goretide has repeatedly failed to learn.`,

  'the-beastbridge': `A natural arch of fused rock carrying the coast road over a Vitrolian chasm, wide enough for a column and old enough that nobody knows whether it was cut or formed. The herds cross it too, which is where the name comes from.

It is the only wheeled route between Vitrolia and the southern coast. Blocking it does not stop an army, but it does stop an army’s supply train.`,

  'polychromatic-sea': `A stretch of water south of Vitrolia where mineral bloom turns the surface into bands of colour that shift through the day. It is beautiful, and it is poisonous to drink and unpleasant to swim in.

The dyes rendered from it are worth more by weight than most metals, and the sea is worked by crews who know exactly what it is doing to them.`,

  'sacred-lake': `A cold, clear lake on the edge of the Flamescar Plateau, improbable in this country and treated as a minor miracle accordingly. Shrines ring the shore and nobody fishes it.

In a realm this dry, a lake that does not steam away in summer is worth a pilgrimage and worth a garrison. The Free Cities have quietly provided both.`,

  // --- Khul's Ravage, the Flamespires and the Vitriol Sea -----------------
  'voyagers-end': `A port town on the western edge of the Ravage, the last safe harbour before the coast turns hostile and the reason for its name. Ships bound east reprovision here and crews who have thought better of the voyage pay off here.

It survives because the Goretide finds it more useful intact than burned — the slave markets further east need somewhere for the ships to come from.`,

  'steel-spike': 'A duardin-built fortress city on the Bridge of Brine, guarding the southern entrance to Aspiria on the eastern edge of the Great Parch, its watchtower driven into a ridge of native iron like a nail into wood to warn of raids out of the Scablands.\n\nGenerations of additions have turned the whole peninsula into a single fortress, defences formidable enough that during the Age of Chaos even Khorne’s warhosts chose to go around Steel Spike rather than test its walls outright. It has fallen and been retaken since by lesser attackers who lacked that sense, often enough that the garrison keeps a tally of the sieges carved into the gate.',

  'vociferous-delta': `A river mouth west of Steel Spike where the outflow roars loud enough to drown conversation on the bank. Signal flags are the only practical way to give orders within a league of it.

That has made the Delta a favoured crossing for anyone who does not want to be heard coming, and a nightmare for the pickets trying to hear them.`,

  'bridge-of-brine': `The salt causeway linking Steel Spike’s peninsula to the mainland, submerged at high water and passable at low. The duardin have reinforced it without ever raising it, deliberately.

An enemy who wants Steel Spike has to time an assault to the tide, in full view of the fortress, twice a day. Nobody has yet made that work.`,

  'obsidian-fortress': `A stronghold cut into a single mass of volcanic glass on the northern Flamespires, black, seamless and impossible to mine under. Whoever built it did not leave a name on it.

It has been occupied by Order, by Chaos and twice by nobody at all. The current garrison flies Tempest’s Eye’s colours and is not asked too closely how it got in.`,

  'tempests-eye': 'Tempest’s Eye is a multilevel Free City carved from the rock of Titanspear Mountain in the Flamespires, south-west of the Flamescar Plateau, its cliffside docks crowded daily with duardin airships and gyrocutters working the winch-lifts and cable-ways that hold the city together.\n\nRaised from the fortified towns of the Realmgate Wars not long after Hammerhal itself, ahead of Hallowheart and Anvilgard both, it sits close enough to the firmament that celestial magic gathers strongly here, filling its planar observatories and arcane orreries with seers and battle-scryers keeping constant watch on the lands below. The Tempest Lords Stormhost made the city their own from the beginning, and no civic or martial edict passes the Grand Conclave without the signatures of Castle Regal’s Lord-Celestant and Lord-Castellant.\n\nThat arrangement gives Tempest’s Eye a readiness few Free Cities can match: a garrison that answers to its own Stormhost, watchtowers that see trouble coming before it arrives, and a position high enough above the Great Parch’s usual wars to make it a base worth holding rather than a battlefield worth winning outright. Enemies who want the Parch’s skies still have to go through it first.',

  'the-flamespires': `The mountain range south-west of the Flamescar Plateau, a line of thin volcanic spires standing over the ash plain like chimneys, one of which Tempest’s Eye is carved into. The updrafts between them are strong enough to carry an airship without engines.

Every aerial route in the southern Parch runs through the Flamespires because the winds make it cheap. That is the range’s whole strategic value, and it is considerable.`,

  sacrifice: `A town on the Ravage’s northern edge that answered a Goretide demand by giving the warlord what he asked for, and was burned anyway. The name on the plate is what the survivors called it afterwards.

The ruins are left standing. Both sides find them useful as a lesson, for opposite reasons.`,

  'goldenmane-monument': `A pillar raised on the Ravage plain over the grave of a warrior the local tribes still name, weathered down to a stump but never pulled over. Korghos Khul’s warhosts march past it without touching it.

Nobody has satisfactorily explained that restraint. The tribes take it as proof the monument matters; the Goretide has not commented.`,

  'khuls-ravage': 'The blood-soaked heartland Korghos Khul carved out for the Goretide after conquering all the land between Vostargi Mont and the Vitriol Sea, its cracked earth stained permanently red by the slaughter that named it. Jagtooth Forts still watch its coasts, built to guard the slave routes that fed Khul’s armies, and the Red Pyramid he raised from a million skulls marks the region’s grim centre.\n\nNo army holds Khul’s Ravage for long, because the Bane of Aqshy considers it his by right and always returns to remind whoever forgets, having first made that point against Vandus Hammerhand’s Stormcast Eternals at the very start of the Realmgate Wars and kept fighting for it ever since. The Ravage has changed hands more times than either side cares to count, and Khul has never once treated any of those losses as final.',

  'bloodwarm-waters': `The sea off the Ravage’s south-western shore, kept at blood heat by vents on the sea floor and stained by whatever the rivers bring down. Nothing edible lives in it any more.

Fleets that anchor here foul their hulls within a week. The Goretide does not maintain a fleet, which means the Bloodwarm Waters inconvenience only the people trying to reach the coast.`,

  'jagtooth-forts': `The chain of coastal strongpoints Korghos Khul raised along the Ravage’s southern shore to guard his slave routes, each within signalling distance of the next. Most are still manned; a few have been taken and held by whoever could.

Cracking the Jagtooth line is the precondition for any landing in the Ravage. It is also the reason nobody has tried one in a generation.`,

  'hammerhand-coast': `The southern shore of the Ravage, named for the Lord-Celestant whose Stormcast came ashore along it at the opening of the Realmgate Wars. The Goretide has never accepted the name and has never managed to replace it.

The beaches are the only viable landing ground on this coast, which is why the Jagtooth Forts watch them and why every Order plan for retaking the Ravage starts here.`,

  'drizzling-isles': `A scatter of islets south of the Ravage under a permanent cap of warm rain, the only reliably wet ground for a hundred leagues. Fishing families hold them and guard the cisterns jealously.

Fresh water within reach of the Ravage coast is a strategic resource. The islanders know it and sell it accordingly.`,

  duardinia: `A duardin holding on the Ravage’s eastern flank, dug in rather than built up, its surface works small and its workings deep. It has been besieged repeatedly and taken never.

The lodges here trade ore south to Fort Ignis and refuse to discuss what they are digging towards. Khul’s warhosts have stopped trying and started going around.`,

  'fort-ignis': `A tower fortress on the Ravage’s southern promontory, holding the coast road where it narrows to a single ledge above the sea. It is small, unlovely and impossible to bypass.

The garrison rotates out of Hammerhal and treats the posting as a punishment. It is still one of the more important pieces of masonry on the Parch, because the road behind it feeds the whole southern front.`,

  'igneous-delta': `Where the Ravage’s meltwater rivers reach the Vitriol Sea through a fan of black basalt channels, still warm enough to steam in the mornings. The land is new, unstable and grows every year.

It is the one soft approach into the Ravage from the sea, which is why Fort Ignis stands where it does.`,

  'brimstone-peninsula': 'A spur of black rock and sulphur vents reaching into Aqshy’s southern sea, named for the choking fumes that roll off it at low tide. It marks the old edge of Khul’s slave routes, once patrolled by the Jagtooth Forts he raised to guard them, and whoever fortifies it still controls the coast road into the Great Parch.\n\nIt is also where the Age of Sigmar itself effectively began: the Whispering Gate, most famous of the Gates of Azyr, opens here, and its recapture by Vandus Hammerhand’s newly forged Stormcast Eternals against Korghos Khul’s own Goretide was the first battle of the Realmgate Wars. Khul survived that defeat and withdrew to build his Ravage further inland, but the peninsula has never stopped being the place where the Stormhosts first proved the God-King’s return was more than a rumour.',

  'mordacious-sound': 'A narrow, storm-wracked channel where two seas of molten ash meet and never quite mix, treacherous to anything short of a duardin ironclad. Salvage crews brave the Sound anyway, since whatever sinks in it is preserved rather than destroyed.',

  'vitriolus-reach': `The southern arm of the Vitriol Sea, where the water turns acid enough to strip a hull to the frames in a season. Crossing it is a calculated expense rather than a risk.

Nothing is built on its shores and nothing needs to be. The Reach is a wall, and both sides of the southern war are content to leave it as one.`,

  'sigmars-bootprints': `A line of deep, roughly foot-shaped depressions in the sea floor off the southern coast, visible from above in clear water and spaced far too widely for anything that walks. The name is the obvious one.

Pilgrims sail out to look. Whether the God-King came this way or the sea floor simply subsided in a pattern, the Free Cities have never felt any urgency to establish.`,

  'vitriol-sea': `The great southern ocean of the Great Parch, corrosive enough that a working hull is a consumable and a fleet is an annual expense. Duardin ironclads and sheathed corsair hulls are the only things that cross it routinely.

Because of that, the Vitriol Sea is the Parch’s southern border in practice as well as on the chart. Armies that could march anywhere still stop at its shore.`,

  // --- The Scablands and the Flamescar Plateau ----------------------------
  'the-breaking-of-the-feast': `A field on the Scablands margin where a peace-feast between two tribal confederations ended the way such things do, marked now by a ring of stones and a name. Neither confederation exists any more.

Local custom forbids camping on it. Armies that ignore the custom report nothing unusual, and the custom persists anyway.`,

  'riven-depths': `A system of fissures splitting the Scablands from below, some of them deep enough that no dropped stone has been heard to land. Skaven use them; so does anything else that prefers the dark.

The Free Cities map the mouths and garrison none of them. There are too many, and the ground between them is worth nothing.`,

  'the-scablands': `A crust of fractured, mineral-crazed hardpan between the Flamescar Plateau and the southern coast, too broken for wheels and too hot for herds. The raids that trouble Steel Spike come out of it.

Nobody holds the Scablands. Several powers claim them, which amounts to claiming responsibility for whatever crawls out.`,

  splitsite: `A town on the southern edge of the Flamescar Plateau, built across a fissure that opened through the middle of it and bridged rather than abandoned. Half the houses are on one side, half on the other, and the arrangement is now a point of civic pride.

It sits on the road between the Plateau and the Scablands, which is why it was worth bridging. The fissure still widens, and the bridges are rebuilt longer each generation.`,

  'the-shards': `A field of upthrust rock plates on the Scablands margin, standing on edge like a dropped pack of slates, each one tall enough to hide a column behind. The ground between them is passable.

Ambushes in the Shards are a local tradition. Steel Spike’s patrols go through in strength or go round.`,

  'sorrow-peaks': `A short, steep range on the western Flamescar Plateau, notable mostly for the wind through the passes, which sounds like what the name says. Mining camps work the lower slopes.

The passes carry the road between Vitrolia and the Plateau. That is the only reason armies come here, and it is reason enough.`,

  'klarikkaz-earthscar': `A trench gouged across the Flamescar Plateau in a single event nobody recorded, straight-sided, twenty leagues long and named for whatever did it. The walls are glassed.

It severs the northern Plateau roads. Every caravan between Edassa and the Caverns of Fulminax either detours a week or pays the bridge tolls, and the toll-holders have grown fat on an accident.`,

  'caverns-of-fulminax': `A cave system under the Plateau where the rock carries a charge, and where storms underground are as common as storms above. Prospectors work it for realmstone in the quiet seasons.

Anything metal carried inside becomes a liability. That has kept the caverns out of Skaven hands so far, which is more than can be said for most holes on the Parch.`,

  vandium: `A Plateau town built around a smelter that has not gone cold in four hundred years, supplying pig iron to Hammerhal and Edassa both. It is grimy, prosperous and entirely uninterested in anything but the furnace.

Take Vandium and two Free Cities start rationing metal within the season. The garrison is accordingly larger than the town’s size suggests.`,

  prismatikos: `A lens of clear volcanic glass set in the Plateau floor, a mile across, that splits the sun into bands across the surrounding ash. Aqshy’s wizards come to read what the light does; the caravans come because the road runs past it.

It is a landmark rather than a stronghold, which is exactly why armies use it to set a rendezvous. More than one battle on the Plateau has begun with two forces arriving at Prismatikos on the same day by coincidence.`,

  edassa: 'A crossroads kingdom on the Flamescar Plateau along the Great Ash Road, held together by a pact of forts and garrison towns funding the roads between three rival city-states, freed long ago by General Kyukain Hammer-Friend, whose descendants have ruled it since.\n\nIts Lionesses, warriors who must first slay a Flamescar lion to join the ranks, trace that rite back to Laudine, first Queen of Edassa, who killed such a beast, wore its pelt as the relic still known as the Mane of Laudine, and raised its orphaned cubs rather than let them starve. Edassa’s rulers still measure good rule by that same balance of might and mercy, proof enough that Edassa does not wait on Sigmar’s Stormhosts to defend itself.',

  'flamescar-plateau': `The high, ash-covered tableland at the centre of the Great Parch, crossed by the Great Ash Road and ringed by everything worth holding in the realm. It is dry, hot, open country where cavalry works and sieges do not.

Whoever controls the Plateau controls the movement of every army on the Parch, because the alternatives all go round. That is why Edassa exists, why Hammerhal garrisons its southern edge, and why the Goretide keeps coming back to it.`,

  // --- Aridian, the Searing Sea and the Charrwind Coast -------------------
  'isle-of-ghouls': `A low island in the northern Searing Sea, uninhabited by anything that will admit to it, avoided by every crew that can afford the extra leagues. Ships that shelter there in a blow do not always land the same number of hands they anchored with.

The name is old and the avoidance is older. Nobody has mounted an expedition to establish what is actually on it, which is its own kind of answer.`,

  'the-juttings': `A field of basalt columns standing out of the Searing Sea like a broken colonnade, some of them a hundred feet proud of the water. The channels between them are deep and unpredictable.

Small craft use the Juttings as cover when a larger hull is chasing them. It works often enough to keep the practice alive.`,

  'the-crimson-twins': `Two red-stone headlands closing the western end of the Searing Sea, matched closely enough that sailors use them as a gate mark. The strait between them is the only sheltered approach to the Golvarian coast from the north.

Both have been fortified at various times and neither is now. The Free Cities keep meaning to fix that.`,

  'cupricon-range': `A copper-veined ridge above the Golvarian coast, its slopes stained green and its ore worked by open cuts visible from the sea. Anvilgard’s Ironweld built its foundries on this metal.

The mines have outlived the city’s independence. Whoever rules Har Kuron still ships Cupricon copper, because the alternative is closing the foundries.`,

  'searing-sea': `The northern ocean of the Great Parch, heated from below and dotted with vents that boil the surface without warning. It is fully navigable and comprehensively unpleasant.

Every cargo moving between the Charrwind Coast, Cotha and the northern territories crosses it, which makes the Searing Sea the busiest water in Aqshy and the one with the highest insurance.`,

  'the-leaping-tongues': `Columns of flame that rise straight out of the Searing Sea off the Charrwind Coast, burning on gas from the vents below and running for weeks at a time. They are visible from Anvilgard on a clear night.

Navigators steer by them and nobody sails between them. The Tongues move a little each season, which keeps the charts interesting.`,

  anvilgard: 'Anvilgard was the City of Scales, a heavily fortified port on Aqshy’s Charrwind Coast where Ironweld forge-lords built their fortunes watching the Golvarian Passage, its winding, shadowed streets kept clear of the encroaching jungle only by a constant defoliant mist.\n\nIts council answered to coin before crown for generations, dominated as much by the Blackscale Coil crime syndicate as by any elected voice, until Morathi-Khaine’s Daughters of Khaine, backed by Idoneth Deepkin and the corsair fleets of the Black Arks, took the city outright. Renamed Har Kuron, it has stood a vassal-citadel of her empire ever since, its Hag Priestesses ruling through Scáthborn patrols and gladiatorial spectacle rather than the old council’s bargains.\n\nLoyalist Anvilgardians still fight under the God-King’s name in the city’s depths and beyond its walls, and every faction that once bought Anvilgard’s loyalty with coin now has to reckon with Morathi’s instead, or find some way to prise the port back out of her grip. Whoever eventually holds Har Kuron controls the same thing Anvilgard’s old council always sold: the only safe passage along Aqshy’s southern coast.',

  'butchers-gorge': `The ravine immediately inland of Har Kuron, carrying the only road from the port up onto the Aridian plain. The name is older than the Daughters of Khaine and was not improved by their arrival.

Everything that leaves Har Kuron for the interior goes through the Gorge. Morathi’s garrison counts it, taxes it, and occasionally decides it is not leaving after all.`,

  'charrwind-coast': `The southern shore of the Searing Sea, scoured by hot offshore winds that strip the paint off a hull and the topsoil off anything unplanted. Har Kuron and its jungle hinterland sit on it.

It is the only coast on the northern Parch with harbours worth the name. That single fact has made it the most fought-over shoreline in the realm.`,

  'grimnirs-firehold': `A Fyreslayer holding on the Aridian coast, smaller than Vostargi Mont and older by their own reckoning, built where a fragment of Grimnir is said to have come down. The lodge does not display its ur-gold to visitors.

It sits astride the coast road between the Charrwind harbours and the interior. The duardin charge for passage and have never yet refused it to anyone who paid.`,

  'sootstain-hills': `Low hills east of the Firehold, black to the roots from centuries of downwind ash, where nothing grows and the herds are driven through rather than pastured. The water is drinkable and that is the entire recommendation.

Armies cross the Sootstain because it is flat and empty. Both facts have been used against them.`,

  'the-forge-anathema': `A forge-complex on the northern Aridian plain that the duardin will not name aloud, worked by somebody and producing something. The smoke is constant and the road in is watched.

The Fyreslayer lodges of Vostargi Mont treat it as a standing grudge rather than a target, which suggests they know what is being made there and have decided not to say.`,

  'anthracite-delves': `Deep coal workings under the Aridian scarp, feeding the foundries on the Charrwind Coast and the smelters at Vandium. The seams are thick, the galleries are old, and the props are older.

Whoever holds the Delves sets the price of iron across the northern Parch. Har Kuron holds them at present, and the price has gone up.`,

  'reaver-wastes': `Open, waterless country in central Aridian where the raiding bands out of the north water their horses at the same three wells, whoever they are riding for that season. Nobody farms it and nobody polices it.

Caravans cross the Wastes with escorts or not at all. The wells are the only fixed points in the region and the only ones ever fought over.`,

  'ruins-of-ahramentia': 'The bones of Ahramentia, a sorcerous city the mage-elite of the Agloraxi once raised to drift above the Great Parch, its libraries and galleries a wonder that kings and warlords crossed the continent to pay tribute before the Arch-Domini.\n\nThe Agloraxi grew bored of ruling and withdrew into their own esoteric works, and their demands for tribute lapsed along with their attention, until Khorne answered their slaughter of a daemon horde with a single furious fist that struck the floating city from the sky. Its wreckage now litters the northern Flamescar Plateau, and treasure-hunters still pick through Ahramentia’s ash for relics of whoever built it, enough of them returning rich that more keep coming.',

  aridian: 'A dust-scoured stretch of the Great Parch where the rivers ran dry generations ago and the wells are worth more than the land around them. Nomads and scavenger companies contest every waterhole, because in Aridian thirst kills faster than any warband does.',

  'vostargi-mont': 'Vostargi Mont is a blackened volcanic massif rising over the Flamescar Plateau, formed where the embers of Grimnir’s own demise fell to earth, its slopes riddled with old mine-workings and older grudges.\n\nThe Vostarg lodge, largest and most powerful of the Fyreslayers and descended from Vostarg himself, foremost of the First-Forged, built their magmahold through the mountain from a mile above the cloud-line down to workings deep beneath the Parch. Centuries of eruption have sheathed much of the slope in Vostargi Obsidian, a volcanic glass valuable enough on its own that duardin prospectors and fire-cultists still contest what the mountain has not yet given up.\n\nNeither side will say what they have found inside it, and the Vostarg are not in the habit of explaining themselves to outsiders who ask. What is certain is that a mountain holding both a Fyreslayer lodge’s ur-gold and a volcano’s memory of a god’s death is never going to run short of people willing to dig for either.',

  'pyroc-plains': `Flat, burning grassland east of Anvalor where the grass ignites on contact in high summer and regrows within a month. The nomads time their movements to the burn.

An army caught on the Pyroc Plains out of season loses its baggage and sometimes more. Local guides are expensive and worth it.`,

  anvalor: 'A garrison town strung along Aqshy’s inland roads, more waystation than city, built to keep the supply lines open between the Great Parch’s Free Cities. It has changed hands more times than its walls have been properly repaired, which suits whoever is currently holding it just fine.',

  'lava-bleed-portal': `A realmgate in eastern Aridian that opens onto somewhere molten, held shut by a ring of duardin-built sluices and bleeding a slow, constant flow of lava down the hillside regardless. The flow is quarried.

Closing it entirely has been attempted twice. Both attempts are commemorated by the shape of the hill.`,

  'splitbrow-lake': `A lake in two halves either side of a knife-edge ridge, one side fresh and one side mineral-poisoned, with no evident reason for the difference. The Aridian tribes use the fresh half and stay well clear of the other.

It is the largest reliable water in eastern Aridian. That makes a lake with a poisoned twin the most strategically important feature for a hundred leagues.`,

  'cruel-spread': `An expanse of thin, stony ground between Anvalor and Capilaria, farmed by people who get one crop in three and stay anyway. The spread is wide, the yields are poor, and the villages are close together for protection.

Armies that march across it strip it bare and the villages do not recover for a decade. Both sides know this and both do it.`,

  'onyx-gate': `A black stone arch on the Aridian road below the Adamantine Chain, a realmgate that has not opened in living memory and is nonetheless guarded. The guard is provided by nobody in particular and maintained regardless.

Its condition is checked monthly by riders out of Attramor. What they are checking for is not written down.`,

  'adamantine-chain': `The mountain range running down the eastern edge of Aridian, hard enough to blunt duardin tools and named for it. It divides the settled Parch from the Gnaw and everything east of it.

There are four passes. The Skaven have undermined two of them, the Fyreslayers hold one, and the fourth is the reason Attramor has walls.`,

  'brazenskull-volcano': `An active cone at the northern end of the Adamantine Chain, erupting on a rough forty-year cycle and named for the shape of the caldera. Ash from it falls as far as the Sootstain Hills.

The Fyreslayers of the Grymhold read the mountain for warning signs and have never been wrong about a date. Everyone downwind pays attention when they start moving.`,

  'the-grymhold': `A fortified duardin hold under the Adamantine Chain, grim even by the standards of a people who do not go in for cheer. It guards the northernmost usable pass.

The Grymhold does not negotiate passage and does not explain refusals. Both sides of the eastern war have learned to route around it, which is precisely the intended effect.`,

  attramor: `A walled town at the mouth of the Adamantine pass, the last Order settlement before the Chain and the market where everything crossing it changes hands. It is prosperous, heavily garrisoned and permanently nervous.

Attramor is the eastern Parch’s early warning. When Attramor’s signal fires go up, Capilaria starts calling in its levies.`,

  'snow-peaks': `The high ground east of Attramor, cold enough to hold snow year-round in defiance of the realm it stands in. The Fyreslayers regard the anomaly as significant and will not say why.

The snowmelt feeds every well on the eastern Aridian plain. Fouling it has been tried, and the Grymhold’s response to the attempt is still discussed.`,

  // --- Magmar Fjords and the northern shore -------------------------------
  'magmar-fjords': 'Deep-cut inlets where molten rock meets the sea and cools into black glass cliffs, treacherous to chart and worse to land on. Fyreslayer lodges prize the ur-gold seams beneath the fjords enough to fight duardin and outsider alike for the rights to mine them.',

  shimfront: `A town on the far northern shore, built where the permanent heat-haze off the water meets the cold air off the sea and hangs as a wall of shimmer. Ships find it by sound rather than sight.

It is the northernmost port on the Parch and the transshipment point for everything coming down from the Cinderfloes. Being hard to see has done nothing to make it less valuable.`,

  drumsden: `A fjord town, burned and not rebuilt, its stone quays still sound and its houses roofless. The fishing fleet that worked out of it is gone with it.

The quays are used. Crews put in to shelter and leave before dark, and nobody has claimed the site, which in this country is unusual enough to be worth noting.`,

  'the-whiptails': `A tangle of narrow channels between the Magmar Fjords and the northern plain, so convoluted that a boat can be a day’s sail from where it started and half a mile away. They freeze at the mouths and never in the middle.

The Whiptails are the only way inland from this coast that does not involve the fjord cliffs. Pilots who know them charge like surgeons.`,

  burningbridge: `A span across a fjord inlet built of fire-hardened timber and maintained by burning it, the charred surface renewed each season because charcoal outlasts wood here. It is the only crossing for thirty leagues.

An army can use it. An army cannot use it twice in quick succession, because the bridge needs a week between burns, and that detail has decided at least one campaign.`,

  'pesten-sludge': `A slow, thick outflow on the northern plain where something upstream has gone very wrong, running grey-green and killing the ground on either bank. It drains out of the Gnaw.

The Skaven upstream are not doing it deliberately. That is generally considered the more alarming reading.`,

  vexport: `A port on the northern coast that has changed owners often enough for the harbourmaster’s ledger to serve as a political history. It ships ore out and grain in.

Whoever holds Vexport feeds the northern territories. The Skaven of the Gnaw have taken it twice and been unable to run it either time, which is the only reason it is still standing.`,

  'pesten-mound': `A raised earthwork on the northern plain, too regular to be natural, venting warm gas through its crown. Nothing grows on it and nothing nests in it.

Digging into the Mound has been attempted. What the digging party found is the reason the current cordon exists.`,

  // --- Hel Crown and the Verminfont ---------------------------------------
  'hel-crown': 'A ring of fused, crown-shaped rock said to mark where a Chaos warlord tried and failed to raise himself a throne. The rock remembers the fire that shaped it, and something in it still draws the ambitious to try where the last one failed.',

  helbridge: `A causeway across the flooded ground east of Hel Crown, built of rubble from the Crown itself and passable in any weather. Whoever built it wanted an army to be able to cross.

It is the fastest route between the Crown and the eastern coast. Order patrols it when it can and concedes it when it cannot, which is most of the time.`,

  'flotillas-scramble': `A stretch of the north-eastern coast where the water shoals without warning and a fleet coming in under sail ends up scattered across two leagues of shallows. It has happened often enough to earn the name.

Any landing here is a landing in pieces. That is the whole of the region’s defence and it has so far been sufficient.`,

  hexeneste: `A walled town on the north-eastern plain, prosperous and unusually intact for its position, doing steady business with neighbours it does not name. Its market takes coin from anyone.

Hexeneste sits close enough to the Verminfont that its continued good health raises questions. The town declines to answer them and keeps trading.`,

  'the-verminfont': `A vast sinkhole on the north-eastern plain, rimmed with spoil and exhaling warm, foul air, out of which the Skaven of the eastern Parch come and into which most of what they take goes. It is a mouth rather than a settlement.

Every campaign against the Gnaw eventually proposes sealing the Verminfont. Nobody has worked out how to seal something that goes down further than anyone has measured, and the attempts have been expensive.`,

  'hels-claw': `A fortress on the ridge east of Hel Crown, taken, held and abandoned so many times that the current ruin is a composite of four different architectures. It commands the ridge road.

Garrisoning it is a standing argument in Hammerhal’s war council. The walls are sound and the water is not, which is how it has fallen every time.`,

  veilt: `A town on the eastern coast that survives by being unremarkable, with no walls worth storming and no wealth worth the march. It fishes, it trades, it endures.

Both sides of the eastern war use Veilt as a place to buy supplies without being asked questions. The town has quietly made that its entire economy.`,

  castbridge: `A duardin-built bridge over the eastern channel, cast rather than cut, in one piece, by a method the builders did not record. It has not needed repair in four hundred years.

It carries the only wheeled route between Hel Crown and the southern coast. Both sides have declined to destroy it, which is either respect or the recognition that they will want it later.`,

  // --- Capilaria and the Heartblood Sea -----------------------------------
  capilaria: 'A cluster of hill-forts and terraced farmland improbably green for the Realm of Fire, fed by mineral springs and once laced with Aqua Ghyranis that let its tribes farm land Vostargi Mont’s eruptions ought to have scoured bare. Sigmar himself favoured the Capillarian tribes on his wanderings through the Great Parch, their hammers reminding him of the World-That-Was.\n\nThat old blessing has not spared Capilaria from ruin: its irrigation networks ran red with gore during the Red Century, choking the same fields they once fed, and the strongpoints raised since, Eonid’s Vengeance, Kismet and Lymeport among them, hold ground that has to be fought for freshly each generation. Every Free City in the Great Parch still wants Capilaria’s harvests, which makes it a battlefield every season it is not a famine.',

  trucebreak: `A Capilarian town razed during a negotiated ceasefire by a party that has never been established, and left as it fell by common agreement. The name is the accusation.

Every peace talk held in Capilaria since has been held in the open, in sight of the ruins, at the insistence of whichever side trusts the other least.`,

  'hammerhal-aqsha': 'Hammerhal Aqsha is the fire-realm half of the Twin-tailed City, raised on the Aqshy side of the Stormrift Realmgate that links it to Hammerhal Ghyra in the Realm of Life. Known as the Bright City for the glow of its furnace-rows, it sprawls across the Great Parch in a permanent haze of smoke and forge-heat, its skyline built of chimneys, foundries and barracks rather than the golden domes across the gate.\n\nThe city rose in the years after the Realmgate Wars, when Vandus Hammerhand’s Hammers of Sigmar and the armies that followed him secured the realmgate and began building on both its faces at once, the first and grandest of the new Free Cities. Hammerhal Aqsha became the Stormhosts’ foremost arsenal, its foundries turning out sigmarite arms and armour without pause, and its Perspicarium, reckoned by many the grandest Stormkeep raised anywhere in the realms, stands as proof of how much weight the city’s forges carry in Sigmar’s wars.\n\nEvery faction that covets the Free Cities starts its ambitions here, because whoever taxes the Stormrift Gate taxes half the traffic between two realms, and whoever arms an army does it with Hammerhal steel. The magistrates on the Aqshan side have grown richer than any Free City council likes to admit, and the furnaces that make that wealth possible are also the reason no enemy of Order has ever stopped dreaming of snuffing them out.',

  'heartblood-sea': `An inland sea east of Hammerhal, red from the iron suspended in it and warm all year, fed by rivers that drain the old Capilarian battlefields. The colour is mineral; the name is not.

It is the shortest route from Hammerhal to the eastern territories and the reason the city maintains a river fleet. Nothing edible lives in it, so everything a garrison on its shore eats comes up the road.`,

  'chalriks-folly': `A half-finished fortress east of Embergard, begun on a scale its builder could not finish and abandoned with the curtain wall complete and nothing inside it. Chalrik’s name survives only in the mockery.

The wall is still a wall. Warbands shelter inside it, which means the Folly has served a defensive purpose after all, just not the intended one.`,

  embergard: `A Capilarian border fortress, taken and burned during the eastern incursions and never reoccupied. Its magazines went up with it and the crater is still visible.

Rebuilding it is proposed at every Hammerhal council and funded at none. The frontier has moved west, and Embergard is now inside somebody else’s territory.`,

  'gates-below': `A set of stone doors set flat into the Capilarian hillside, opening downwards onto stairs that go further than anyone has followed them. The doors are duardin work and predate the lodges that claim them.

They are guarded from the outside, which tells you which direction the guards expect trouble from.`,

  skulpile: `A heap of bone on the eastern Capilarian plain, accumulated rather than raised, marking the edge of the Goretide’s deepest push in the Red Century. It is still growing.

Order patrols count it. An increase means somebody is fighting upstream, which is worth knowing a week before the refugees arrive.`,

  'the-embergard-incursions': `The corridor of ruined country running south-east from Embergard, marking where the eastern warbands came through and how far they got. Villages along it are abandoned, fortified or both.

It is the standing invasion route into Capilaria. Hammerhal’s forces know it, plan for it, and have never been able to close it.`,

  'swelter-gulf': `A dead-calm bay on the south-eastern coast where the heat sits on the water and the air does not move for weeks. Sailing craft are becalmed in it routinely; oared and engined hulls are not.

That gives duardin ironclads and Skaven rafts the run of the Gulf and denies it to everyone else. It is a small advantage and it has decided more than one coastal campaign.`,

  'emberkell-volcano': `An active cone south of Hammerhal, close enough that the city’s eastern districts sweep ash off their roofs as a matter of routine. Its eruptions are frequent, modest and closely watched.

Hammerhal’s foundries draw heat from its flanks through duardin-built channels. The arrangement is efficient and everybody involved is aware of what happens if the mountain does something out of character.`,

  'chronos-gate': `A realmgate on the southern Capilarian coast that opens reliably and returns travellers at inconsistent intervals. Going through is straightforward; coming back is a negotiation with the arithmetic.

The Free Cities use it for dispatches they do not need urgently and for people they are willing to lose track of. Nobody marches an army through a gate that might deliver it late.`,

  'caustic-peaks': `A ridge of sulphur-yellow mountains east of Emberkell, the rock crumbling and the runoff poisonous. Nothing is mined here because nothing survives the mining.

The Peaks screen Capilaria’s southern approach. An army that wants Hammerhal from the south either crosses them and arrives sick, or goes round and arrives late.`,

  'the-horn-of-ignax': `A spur of red rock on the southern coast, curved and tapering, named for the godbeast whose passage through Aqshy left marks all over the eastern realm. Whether Ignax ever touched it is a matter of faith.

Pilgrims land at the base and climb. The Free Cities tolerate the traffic because the pilgrims pay harbour dues at Fort Denst on the way.`,

  'fort-denst': `A squat coastal fort on the southern Capilarian shore, built to watch the Settled Lands approach and enlarged twice since without ever becoming handsome. It holds the harbour and the road behind it.

Denst is the southern hinge of Hammerhal’s coastal defence. If it goes, the Heartblood road is open and the city starts fighting on two fronts.`,

  'the-fyretines': `A line of narrow rock fingers running out into the southern sea, each one a hazard and collectively a barrier. The channels between them are navigable by small craft in daylight.

They shelter the approach to Everlyme Point from the west, which is the only reason that settlement has never been raided from the sea.`,

  // --- The Settled Lands and the Clavis isles -----------------------------
  'the-settled-lands': `The stretch of the south-eastern coast that was farmed, walled and taxed while the rest of the eastern Parch was still being fought over, and which has kept the name out of a certain smugness. The ground is good and the harvests are reliable.

That prosperity is why Renstan’s Keep exists and why the Settled Lands have been invaded three times in living memory. Quiet, fertile country next to a war is not a stable arrangement.`,

  'rondel-wick': `A small harbour town on the Settled Lands coast, sheltered behind a ring of rock, with one quay and a reputation for asking no questions. It provisions anything that comes in.

Both the Free Cities’ patrols and the people they are patrolling for put in at Rondel Wick. The town has made an art of not having them meet.`,

  'ripplegnash-sound': `A channel on the eastern coast where the tide runs hard over a bed of broken rock and chews through anchor cable. Anchoring in the Sound is a way of losing a ship slowly.

It is nonetheless the fastest passage between the Settled Lands and the Clavis isles, and everyone in a hurry uses it.`,

  'renstans-keep': `A stone keep on the Settled Lands coast, the fortification the region built when it stopped pretending it was safe. It is modern, well sited and adequately garrisoned.

Renstan’s Keep answers to the Settled Lands rather than to any Free City, which makes it a useful ally and an unreliable one. It defends its own ground first.`,

  'clavis-rift': `A fissure running north from the Eye across the sea floor and up onto the isles, still open and still venting. It is the reason the Aqshy Clavis Isles exist at all.

Wizards study it from a distance. The distance is set by experience.`,

  'the-eye': 'A volcanic caldera so wide its far rim is lost in heat-haze, still active enough that its glow lights the horizon for leagues. Whole cults have made the climb to its lip and not come back the same, if they came back at all.',

  'cape-of-spines': `A headland on the southern approach to the Settled Lands, fringed with upthrust rock needles that make a landing impossible and a passage uncomfortable. It is the last mark before the open Vitriol Sea.

Nobody holds the Cape. Everybody navigates by it, which makes its lighthouse the most quietly important structure on this coast.`,

  'aqshy-clavis-isles': `An archipelago south of the Eye, thrown up by the Clavis Rift and still settling, its islands appearing and subsiding within a lifetime. Charts carry dates rather than certainties.

Fishing families work them and rebuild elsewhere when an island goes. Nobody fortifies ground that may not be ground next year.`,

  'everlyme-point': `A settlement on the southern tip of the Clavis chain, built around the kilns that burn the local rock into the lime the whole eastern coast builds with. The smoke is constant and the trade is steady.

Everlyme mortar holds Fort Denst together and half the Settled Lands besides. A blockade here is felt slowly and then all at once.`,

  'isthmus-lash': `A narrow neck of land running out into the Vitriol Sea, flogged by the surf from both sides and reduced a little more every year. It will not be an isthmus for many more generations.

While it lasts it carries a footpath. Armies do not use it and smugglers do.`,

  'dagger-star': `A single thin island east of the Settled Lands, straight-sided and pointed, with a beacon on the tip that has been kept burning by someone for as long as anyone can remember. The keepers are not from anywhere.

Every chart of the eastern coast is oriented on the Dagger Star’s light. Whoever is keeping it has made themselves indispensable without ever asking for anything.`,

  // --- The Gnaw -----------------------------------------------------------
  'the-northern-incursions': `The band of broken, tunnelled country along the far northern coast where the Skaven have come up and the surface has not recovered. Villages are gone; the roads that served them end at holes.

It is the northern face of the Gnaw’s expansion. Order has no presence here and no immediate plan to acquire one.`,

  ratpile: `A spoil heap on the northern Gnaw the size of a hill, made of everything the tunnels below have pushed out, and colonised by what lives in it. The heap is still growing.

Surveyors use its height to estimate how much has been dug out beneath. The estimate is the reason Order stopped surveying.`,

  creepbleak: `A stretch of the northern Gnaw where the ground has subsided into a shallow, featureless pan of grey mud, and where anything that crosses it is visible for miles. Nothing grows.

It is the only open ground in the region, which makes it the only place an army can form up and the last place anyone wants to.`,

  'endless-waste': `The interior of the Gnaw’s northern reach, undermined so thoroughly that the surface has become a crust over a void, dotted with collapses. There is no safe route across it and no reason to want one.

The Skaven do not need the surface here and do not defend it. That is the only thing keeping the Waste from being a front line.`,

  'parasite-crawl': `A settlement on the Gnaw plain, taken over from the inside rather than stormed, its buildings still standing and its population replaced. The chimneys still smoke.

It is used as a waypoint by Skaven columns moving east. Order scouts who have looked closely enough to report have mostly not come back to do it twice.`,

  'skryredoom-wastes': `Country poisoned by whatever Clan Skryre has been testing in it, the ground glassed in patches and the air wrong on still days. It expands after each test.

The Wastes are a measure of Skryre’s output. Watching the boundary move is the cheapest intelligence the Free Cities get out of the Gnaw.`,

  'the-gnaw': 'A honeycombed badland east of the Adamantine Chain, torn into the Great Parch during the Vermindoom at the close of the Era of the Beast, when the Skaven dragged a fragment of Blight City itself into the Realm of Fire.\n\nWhat is left is a warren of collapsed tunnels and sinkholes that Clan Mors and Clan Skryre both still claim as their own, digging further beneath the Great Parch with every season. Surface-dwellers who fall through the crust rarely explain what they saw below, which tells the rest of Aqshy everything it needs to know about staying well clear of the hole.',

  'warpstone-spoil': `The open workings where the Gnaw’s warpstone comes out of the ground, worked in the open because the Skaven have long since stopped caring what the exposure does to the workforce. The glow is visible at night from the Adamantine passes.

This is what the whole Gnaw is for. Every tunnel, every incursion and every collapsed village on this half of the plate exists to keep the Spoil producing.`,

  'infestine-coast': `The Gnaw’s eastern shoreline, a maze of flooded channels and undercut cliffs where the tunnels have broken through to the sea. The water goes in and does not visibly come out.

Ships do not close this coast. The one survey that tried is the reason the Free Cities have an outline of the Gnaw’s eastern edge and no interest in a second.`,

  deepneste: `A Skaven surface-hold on the Gnaw plain, the visible portion of something that goes down for miles, ringed with spoil and rebuilt continuously. It is a chimney rather than a city.

Every Order plan for a strike into the Gnaw names Deepneste as the objective. None of them has explained what holding a hole would achieve.`,

  'the-scurry': `The stretch of eastern Gnaw coast across which the Skaven move between the warrens and their shipping, a churned corridor of trampled ground that never has time to recover. Traffic on it is constant.

It is the Gnaw’s supply line to the sea. Cutting it is the single most useful thing an eastern fleet could do and nobody has yet been willing to sail close enough to try.`,

  moulderport: `The Gnaw’s harbour, such as it is: a set of rotting jetties and pens on the eastern shore from which Clan Moulder ships out what it grows and brings in what it needs to keep growing it. The smell reaches the roads.

It is the only place on the eastern seaboard where the Gnaw is vulnerable to a fleet. Every admiral in the Free Cities has drawn up the raid, and none of them has been given the ships.`,

  // --- Phosphoria and the eastern isles -----------------------------------
  'pepper-woad': `A scrubland on the north-eastern islands where the native growth burns the skin of anyone who wades through it and is harvested anyway, because the powder it yields is worth its weight. Harvesters work in leather and still come out marked.

Phosphoria’s merchants control the trade and have grown rich on a crop nobody can steal without knowing how to handle it.`,

  'the-ignaxi-incursions': `The country on the eastern islands where the godbeast Ignax has passed and left the ground changed, in tracks that cut across older geography without regard for it. New ones appear.

The local powers do not fight over Ignaxi ground. They wait to see whether it stays where it is.`,

  'scales-of-the-solar-drake': `A chain of overlapping plate-like islands running down the far eastern edge of the plate, each one rimmed with a ridge and hollowed in the middle, named for what they resemble from the air. The resemblance is exact enough to have started arguments.

They shelter a navigable inside passage down the eastern coast. That is worth more to the fleets of the Ocean of Tears than the question of what they are.`,

  'dread-reach': `A long, exposed arm of the eastern coast facing the open Ocean of Tears, with no harbour, no shelter and a reputation earned over centuries. Ships stand well off it.

The Reach is the eastern Parch’s seaward boundary. Nothing comes at Phosphoria from this direction, which is the only reason Phosphoria can face west.`,

  'ocean-of-tears': `The eastern ocean, cold by Aqshy’s standards and largely empty, stretching off the plate towards coasts nobody on the Parch has charted. It is crossed rarely and deliberately.

What little is known of it comes from the Phosphorian traders who work its western margin. They are not forthcoming, which has kept their routes profitable.`,

  'red-contention': `A tangle of islands and channels between the Gnaw and Phosphoria that both claim and neither controls, fought over island by island and never decisively. Ownership is seasonal.

Whoever holds the Contention holds the sea route between the eastern warrens and the eastern ports. That is why it has never been allowed to settle.`,

  'godbeasts-bite': `A semicircular bay on the Phosphorian coast, scooped out of the headland in one event, with the missing material nowhere to be found. Local reckoning attributes it to Ignax and does not dwell on it.

It is the best natural harbour in eastern Aqshy. New Plunder was built on it, and what happened to New Plunder has not stopped anyone else wanting it.`,

  'new-plunder': `A port on the Godbeast’s Bite, founded by people who wanted the best harbour on the eastern coast and sacked by people who wanted it more. The name was optimistic even before the burning.

The harbour is unaffected. Somebody will rebuild, because a bay this good does not stay empty, and the next town will be walled.`,

  'dreadfort-spines': `A ridge of fortified stone teeth running down Phosphoria’s spine, each one a strongpoint, built by an authority that no longer exists. They are individually small and collectively formidable.

Phosphoria’s current powers occupy the ones they can supply and leave the rest. An enemy advancing along the ridge has to take them in order, which is the whole point.`,

  phosphoria: `The eastern province, a dry country of pale burning rock and mineral flats that glows faintly on moonless nights. It is thinly settled, mineral-rich and a long way from anyone who could help.

Phosphoria trades east across the Ocean of Tears and fights west against the Gnaw, and its loyalties to the Free Cities are correspondingly theoretical. It will hold the line as long as the line is profitable.`,

  sulphuria: 'A yellow, choking flatland where the ground weeps sulphur and nothing grows that was not built to survive it. Salamander-hunters and Fyreslayer prospectors are the only ones who go there willingly, and both know better than to trust the ground underfoot.',

  deadport: `A harbour town on the Phosphorian coast, still working, still inhabited, and named for the state of the water rather than the place. Nothing lives in the anchorage.

It is the last friendly port before the Sulphurian ground begins. Every expedition east reprovisions at Deadport and a good number of them do not come back through it.`
};
